.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
.TH PKGRM 8 "" "imai #VERSION#" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
pkgrm \- remove an installed CRUX software package
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pkgrm
.RI [ options ] " package"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pkgrm
removes a package by its installed name, not by an archive filename.
Root privileges are required. Files owned by another package under the exact
same inventory spelling are retained. Directories are removed only if empty.
Final symlinks are unlinked rather than followed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-root " " path
Select another system's root filesystem and package database instead of /.
This is not a package relocation option or a sandbox.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the invoked command name, imai identity and version, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.SH OPERATION
The database directory is exclusively locked through removal and database
finalization. Paths are removed in reverse inventory order. Shared ownership
uses original strings, not canonical filesystem identities: distinct spellings
of the same filesystem path are not treated as shared ownership.
The host /sbin/ldconfig helper is run for the selected root when etc/ld.so.conf
exists; its nonzero exit status is ignored for pkgutils compatibility.
Helper setup, execution or wait failures return failure without skipping
best-effort database finalization. Unlike the corresponding pkgutils failure
paths, these failures do not deliberately leave the original package record
after file removal. Database write failures are still possible and reported.
.PP
All selected inventory names are checked before mutation. Empty or absolute
names and parent traversal components are rejected. Required Linux root-scoped
lookup protections have no weaker fallback; magic-link traversal is refused.
The final database file may not be a symlink for mutation.
.PP
Selected objects remain within the operation's authority even if another
process subsequently relocates them outside the pinned root. This is not a
promise of continuous structural containment. See
.BR pkgadd (8)
for the installation trust model and verification limitations.
.PP
Removal is nontransactional. Catchable HUP, INT, QUIT and TERM requests during
mutation are deferred until best-effort completion and database finalization,
then reported without changing the settled operation result. This is not
rollback; SIGKILL, crashes and power loss cannot be deferred.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero preserves the legacy result policy, including after individual file
removal failures. For example, a nonempty directory can remain while the
package record is removed and the command returns zero. Read diagnostics;
status zero does not prove complete removal. Argument, lookup-protection,
preflight and database errors normally return nonzero.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/pkg/db
Package database under the selected root.
.TP
.I /var/lib/pkg/db.backup
Previous database snapshot.
.TP
.I /var/lib/pkg/db.incomplete_transaction
Intermediate database output, potentially partial after failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pkgadd (8),
.BR pkginfo (8),
.BR pkgmk (8),
.BR rejmerge (8)
